The '''Global Maritime Forum''' is an international not-for-profit organisation for the global maritime industry. It convenes leaders from across the maritime community with policy-makers, experts, NGOs and other influential decision-makers.

==Initiatives==	
The [[Getting to Zero Coalition]] is a partnership between the Global Maritime Forum and the [[World Economic Forum]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Getting to Zero Coalition |url=https://www.globalmaritimeforum.org/getting-to-zero-coalition |website=www.globalmaritimeforum.org |access-date=4 October 2022}}</ref> Launched at the [[United Nations Climate Change conference|United Nations Climate Change Conference]] in New York in 2019,<ref>{{cite web |title=Total joins the "Getting to Zero Coalition" to Contribute to Shipping Industry's Decarbonization |url=https://totalenergies.com/media/news/press-releases/total-joins-getting-zero-coalition-contribute-shipping-industrys |website=TotalEnergies.com |date=8 June 2020 |access-date=4 October 2022 |language=en}}</ref> the Getting to Zero Coalition is a platform that convenes around 200 stakeholders from across the shipping and fuels value chain.<ref>{{cite web |title=Getting to Zero Coalition |url=https://www.weforum.org/projects/getting-to-zero-coalition |website=World Economic Forum |access-date=4 October 2022 |language=en}}</ref> The goal of the Getting to Zero Coalition is to have commercially viable Zero Emission Vessels operating along deep sea trade routes by 2030, supported by the necessary infrastructure for scalable zero-carbon energy sources including production, distribution, storage and bunkering.<ref>{{cite web |title=Getting to Zero Coalition - Climate Initiatives Platform |url=https://climateinitiativesplatform.org/index.php/Getting_to_Zero_Coalition |website=climateinitiativesplatform.org |access-date=4 October 2022}}</ref> The ultimate ambition of the Getting to Zero Coalition is to reach the full decarbonization of global shipping by 2050; in 2021, Call to Action initiative was developed with more than 150 industry leaders and organizations urging governments and global shipping industry to commit to full decarbonization of international shipping by 2050.<ref>{{cite news |title=Over 150 industry players join call to action for shipping decarbonization |url=https://www.offshore-energy.biz/over-150-industry-players-join-call-to-action-for-shipping-decarbonization/ |work=Offshore Energy |date=22 September 2021}}</ref>

==Founding partners==
The 14 founding partners of the Global Maritime Forum are [[A.P. Møller-Mærsk]], Arshiya Limited, [[Euronav]], [[Ceres Hellenic Shipping Enterprises|GasLog]], Gorrissen Federspiel, Gulf Agency Company, Heidrick & Struggles, [[Hempel Group]], [[KfW|KfW IPEX-Bank]] [[MISC Berhad|MISC]], [[Onassis Foundation]], [[PSA International]], [[Trafigura]] and [[Wan Hai Lines]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://maritimedanmark.dk/?Id=35869|title=Danmark bag ny global maritim platform|language=Danish|publisher=Maritime Danmark|accessdate=13 September 2017}}</ref>

==Secretariat==
The organization is based in [[Copenhagen]].

==Summits==
The first summit of the Global Maritime Forum took place in Hong Kong, October 2018.{{fact|date=August 2022}}

